// ledger_stub.templ — Zone C, docked.
//
// THERE IS NO DRAWER. The Ledger IS the drawer, permanently docked, so clicking
// a day repaints a panel that was already on screen: nothing appears from
// nowhere and nothing reflows. That is why canon D9's 480px slide-in drawer
// clause was STRUCK (canon amendment A3) rather than reinterpreted, and it is
// the reason this direction won its judging round.
//
// WAVE 1 SHIPS THE ZONE, NOT THE CONTENT. The `needs backend` chip is a SIGNED
// HONESTY STATE, not a shortcut — see the stills, where it appears as a neutral
// chip rather than as a fabricated zero. What matters for wave 1 is that the
// zone is DOCKED AT ITS REAL WIDTH: the full-tier column arithmetic subtracts
// 300px for it unconditionally, so a Block that skipped the zone would measure
// its own columns wrong and flip density at the wrong host width. W-B fills it
// and must not have to re-cut the geometry.
//
// NeedsBackend GATES the chip (data.go ruling): W-B sets it false when the
// Ledger is filled, and must not have to edit this template to stop the chip.

package calendar_block

templ ledgerStub(d BlockData) {
	<div class="ledger" data-zone="ledger">
		<div class="lhead">
			<span class="t">Ledger</span>
			<span class="c">{ d.Month.Name }</span>
			<span class="sp"></span>
			if d.Ledger.NeedsBackend {
				<span class="badge need">needs backend</span>
			}
		</div>
		<div class="lrows">
			<div class="lzero">
				The day answer lands here.
				<br/>
				The Ledger lists by ordinal day, so it works before eras are defined.
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
}
